.TH CTPOOL_FUTURE_FREE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ctpool_future_free \- release the caller's reference to a future
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadpool.h>

void ctpool_future_free(ctpool_future *f);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Drops one reference to the future. When the reference count reaches
zero, the future object is freed with the standard free(). Three kinds
of holder release a reference: the caller, the worker, and every
ctpool_future_get call that is still in progress. For a detached
future the producer holds one in place of the worker.

The call never touches the void* result of the task function, which
this module never allocates, copies or frees. ctpool_future_get(3)
says who owns the result.

Call it exactly once for each successful ctpool_submit_future,
ctpool_try_submit_future, ctpool_timed_submit_future or
ctpool_future_create_detached call, whether ctpool_future_get was
called or not.

If you call it and never call ctpool_future_get, you get
fire-and-forget behavior: the task still runs, and the future is freed
automatically when the worker finishes, with the result thrown away.

You can call it while other threads are inside ctpool_future_get on
the same future. Each of them holds its own reference, so the future
is destroyed only after the last of them leaves.

The call ends the caller's claim on f, as a thread giving up its last
reference does. The caller must not use f again after the call, and
must not hand f to a thread that is not inside ctpool_future_get yet.

A call to ctpool_future_free with a NULL pointer does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
f   Future handle
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ctpool_submit_future (3),
.BR ctpool_try_submit_future (3),
